  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 101 PM MST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nCurrent satellite imagery shows high clouds trekking across the NE\nplains of Colorado. The upper air pattern features slight ridging\naloft as we wait for the shortwave to move in off the coast of \nCalifornia. Temperatures this afternoon across the South Platte \nRiver valley may only reach the lower 40s compared to mid/upper \n40s in the Denver metro as a result of this mornings fog and low \nclouds. A Denver cyclone will develop today and coupled with \nsoutherly downsloping winds off the Palmer Divide, generate breezy\nconditions for Elbert, Lincoln, and areas east of DIA. Low \ntemperatures for the next few nights will reach the 20s with \nvalleys and naturally colder spots approaching the upper teens.\nFreezing fog is also possible Monday morning for Lincoln and\neastern Elbert counties. Reduced visiblities down to 0.25-0.50 sm\nare possible. \n\nEnsemble and deterministic models are in good agreement for a \nshortwave to move across the Central Rockies tomorrow. This \nsystem should bring enough lift and moisture to the mountains to \nproduce our next chance of snow for the higher terrain (40-60%). \nSnowfall is likely to accumulate in the Mosquito Range, Gore \nRange, with lighter accumulations across the Continental Divide \nand Front Range. Snow will start Monday afternoon before tapering\noff early Tuesday morning. The greatest accumulation's appear to \nbe focused on the Gore and Mosquito ranges, where the probability \nof 24 hr snowfall >1\" is 60-70% and >2\" is ~40-50%. Portions of \nthe Palmer Divide and Lincoln county still have a ~10-20% chance \nto see measurable snowfall. Due to the expected cloud cover \ntomorrow, forecasted high temperatures should remain in the lower \n40s for most of the area. Tuesday's sky will clear with cool high \ntemperatures once again in the upper 40s. \n\nUpper-level ridging creeps its way back into Colorado Wednesday as \nthe axis should be centered across our area by the afternoon. \nEnsemble guidance is in good agreement this will induce gradual \nwarming through the weekend. Thanksgiving day looks to be mild in \nthe 50s and dry with no impactful weather. Ensembles for the weekend \ndepict a large high pressure to develop over the western coast causing\nNW flow aloft for the weekend. This NW direction will lead to\nupslope flow for the northern and central mountains which may\nproduce periods of snow showers. Ensembles are in disagreement \nregarding the location of jets and other factors that could \ninfluence snowfall amounts. For now this will be something to \njust keep an eye on. However, our lower elevations are looking \nlikely to stay dry through this weekend.",
